Other Notable Gaps

• Server-side faceted search / advanced catalog filters — Current storefront grid uses client-side JS filtering (search, category, type, attributes). Works for small catalogs but not scalable.
• Invoices & tax reports — Tax reporting code fields exist, but no PDF invoices, tax summary exports, or VAT OSS/MOSS tooling.
• Stock reservation / holds — Stock is reduced on order creation (configurable), but there's no explicit cart-level reservation system.
• Admin bulk actions depth — Modern filters are excellent, but bulk operations (edit, delete, fulfill, email) are limited compared to mature plugins.
• Loyalty / points / referrals — Not present.
• Webhook system maturity — There's a webhooks table + delivery logs, but it appears more for developers than a turnkey "send order.created etc." system.
• Headless / modern API extras — Excellent REST coverage, but no GraphQL and limited official headless guidance.

What's Actually Surprisingly Strong

• Variable products + bundles (with dynamic switching)
• Multi-item cart + checkout (REST + JS driven)
• Reviews (submission + moderation + editing)
• Customer access grants + secure downloads
• Abandoned cart recovery (cron + manual)
• Refunds (UI + gateway refund methods for Stripe/PayPal/Razorpay/etc.)
• Inventory logs + low stock alerts
• Trash/soft-delete everywhere
• Admin UX (filters, modern cards, activity log, customer timelines)
• Upsells / cross-sells (saved + displayed on PDP and checkout)

Summary Recommendation

Biggest gaps for most users right now (in order):

1. Subscriptions (big for digital products — courses, memberships, software updates)
2. Multi-currency
3. Proper shipping rate calculation (if you ever want to sell physical or hybrid)
4. Import (symmetry with the existing export)
5. More powerful promotions beyond basic coupons